# snapshield

[![npm version](https://img.shields.io/npm/v/snapshield.svg)](https://www.npmjs.com/package/snapshield)
[![license: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-blue.svg)](./LICENSE)
[![node](https://img.shields.io/badge/node-%3E%3D16-brightgreen.svg)](https://nodejs.org)
[![PRs welcome](https://img.shields.io/badge/PRs-welcome-orange.svg)](./README.md)

A safety net for AI coding agents. Wrap `claude`, `codex`, `cursor-agent`, or
anything else you point at your repo, and get an instant undo if it breaks
something — even if it ran `git reset --hard`, deleted files, or nuked your
working tree.

## Why

Coding agents occasionally do genuinely destructive things: hard resets,
force pushes, deleted files, mangled merges. This is a tiny, dependency-free
CLI that snapshots your **entire** working tree (tracked, staged, and
untracked-but-not-gitignored files) into hidden commit objects before the
agent runs — so "undo" is always one command away, no matter what the agent
did.

It never touches your real branch, index, or working tree until you
explicitly `restore` — snapshots live in a separate ref namespace
(`refs/snapshield/*`) so they're invisible to normal git commands and safe
from garbage collection.

## Install

```bash
cd snapshield
npm link      # makes the `snapshield` command available globally
# or: npm install -g .
```

(No install step required to just try it — you can always run
`node /path/to/snapshield/bin/snapshield.js ...` directly.)

## Quick start

```bash
cd your-project        # must be a git repo with at least one commit
snapshield init

# Wrap an agent session — this is the main event:
snapshield run -- claude
snapshield run -- codex
snapshield run -- cursor-agent

# Didn't like what it did?
snapshield undo
```

## All commands

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `snapshield init` | One-time setup check in the current repo |
| `snapshield snapshot ["msg"]` | Take a manual checkpoint right now |
| `snapshield list [-n N]` | Show recent snapshots |
| `snapshield diff <id>` | Show what's changed since snapshot `<id>` |
| `snapshield restore <id> [--clean]` | Roll back to snapshot `<id>` (auto-backs-up current state first; `--clean` also removes untracked files added since) |
| `snapshield undo` | Shortcut: roll back to the checkpoint before the most recent one |
| `snapshield run -- <cmd...>` | Snapshot, run `<cmd>`, snapshot again, print a diffstat + undo hint |
| `snapshield watch [--interval SEC]` | Auto-snapshot every SEC seconds (default 120) until Ctrl+C — run in a spare terminal tab during a long agent session |

## How it works (the short version)

Each snapshot is a real git commit object built from a throwaway index (via
`GIT_INDEX_FILE`), so it captures tracked + staged + untracked files without
touching your actual working tree or index. It's parented to your current
`HEAD` and pinned with a ref under `refs/snapshield/snap-N` so git won't
garbage collect it. `restore` always takes a fresh "just in case" snapshot
of whatever state you're in *before* rolling back, so restoring is itself
undoable.

## Known limitations (read before you trust it blindly)

- **Local-machine only.** Snapshots live in your local `.git` directory and
  are not pushed anywhere. If your only copy of the repo is destroyed
  (disk failure, `rm -rf` on the whole project including `.git`), snapshield
  can't help — it protects against bad *commands*, not bad *disks*.
- Requires the repo to already have at least one commit.
- `restore` without `--clean` won't remove files the agent created *after*
  the snapshot; it tells you what those are so you can decide.
- This is a fast prototype, not a security boundary — it hasn't been
  audited or used in production. Try it on low-stakes repos first.

